{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/diagonal-line-extrapolation.md", "content": "# Sparse motif: extrapolating a marked diagonal

A solved test contained a uniform background (8), two same-colored cells (2) at `(6,3)` and `(7,4)`, and nearby differently colored cells at `(7,5)` and `(8,5)`. The output extended only the color-2 diagonal backward with step `(-1,-1)` until the left grid boundary, adding 2s at `(5,2)`, `(4,1)`, and `(3,0)`. The original cells, including the other colors, remained unchanged.

Reusable inference: identify repeated same-color offsets in a small seed; test whether that vector defines a ray/line extended through background. Track direction and clipping against grid edges. Do not propagate unrelated adjacent colors or fill the entire diagonal without evidence. In this conversation, the demos were heterogeneous and mostly identity examples, so they did not establish the test's exact rule; the correct prediction came from recognizing the two-cell same-color diagonal and preserving all unrelated cells. This is a case note, not a universal transformation claim.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/seen/play/users/u046/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/diagonal-line-extrapolation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/consecutive-anchor-gap-lesson.md", "content": "# Consecutive-anchor gap inference

When sparse marker cells define row- or column-wise fill, compare candidate semantics against complete demonstrations. In particular, distinguish filling strictly between consecutive markers from filling the entire interval between the global minimum and maximum coordinates. The latter incorrectly recolors cells beyond an isolated terminal marker. Preserve markers and cells outside demonstrated gaps. After an incorrect submission, identify the precise overgeneralized region, revise the rule, then serialize the candidate grid from the revised model rather than repeating the previous output.
